   The truncation and payload-shape controls are fail-open on command errors. `grep -q ... && abort` treats `grep` exit 2 like “marker absent,” while `grep -c ... || true` masks errors; with `set -uo pipefail` but no `-e`, a transient read/tool failure can proceed to Codex.  
   **Minimal fix:** distinguish grep exit 1 from all other exits, check every `grep`/`wc` assignment explicitly, or enable `set -e` and narrowly disable it only while capturing `codex exec`’s expected nonzero status.

